# 0002. lazy.nvim, pinned versions, lazy loading

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-05

## Context
The config runs on a Mac and an Arch desktop. The old setup pinned nothing, so a fresh install
pulled whatever upstream had that day; treesitter broke this way. Startup speed matters: nvim is
opened constantly from the shell and tmux.

## Decision
- Plugins are managed by lazy.nvim, one spec file per theme in `lua/plugins/`.
- `lazy-lock.json` pins every plugin and is committed with every plugin change; `make sync`
  restores exactly those versions, `make update` is the only thing that moves them.
- Everything that can is lazy-loaded (`keys`, `cmd`, `ft`, `event`). `lazy = false` only when a
  plugin must exist at start, with the reason in a comment. Budget: ~25 ms (`make startup`).
- No background update checks (`checker.enabled = false`).

## Alternatives
Not recorded at the time.

## Rationale
Same tested set on every machine; updates are deliberate and reviewable in a diff.
Lazy loading keeps start time flat as plugins are added.

## Consequences
- Lazy-loaded plugins hide errors until triggered; testing has to load them: [0011](0011-headless-make-check.adr).
- Each machine needs `make sync` after a pull that changed the lockfile.

## References
- `lua/config/lazy.lua`, `Makefile`, README "Earlier review"
